WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.800
Many social animals, like humans,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.340
have large brains and sophisticated cognition.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.320
Is this true for social carnivores like lions and hyenas

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.159
or are solitary meat-eaters like leopards and tigers

00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:14.889
just as smart?

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:17.029
To get the treat from this puzzle box,

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:19.179
the Hyenas didn’t take long to figure out

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:21.820
they needed to pull the rope.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.340
Lions came in second. Some spent time exploring

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.340
the box, which is also indicative of having smarts.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.700
This leopard is also a busy explorer, and scores.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.120
But only 6 of the 11 leopards tested opened the box.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:41.240
Tigers came in last. Only two of these seven

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.800
big cats, like this one, opened the door.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.340
The task doesn’t require being socially intelligent.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.140
Yet, the group-living carnivores were better at it.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.920
That suggests that there’s something about

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.040
being social that makes us smarter overall.

